success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Short answer: they reduce NAV but are excluded from the expense ratio.**\n\n- **Brokerage commissions** are capitalized into the cost basis of securities bought and deducted from sale proceeds. They appear as lower realized or unrealized gains, not fund expenses. Under Form N-1A, dollar commissions are disclosed in the SAI, and portfolio turnover in the prospectus, which notes these costs aren't in the fee table.\n- **Bid-ask spreads and market impact** are implicit in execution prices, never itemized, and simply lower NAV.\n- **Net effect:** These costs show up as tracking difference beyond the expense ratio, so true cost exceeds the reported ratio.\n- **ETF mitigants:**\n  - In-kind creations/redemptions shift most rebalancing trading to authorized participants.\n  - Creation/redemption fees or cash-in-lieu charges offset flow-driven trading costs, protecting existing shareholders.\n  - Low-turnover index funds incur minimal costs. Higher-turnover or active ETFs, especially cash-creation ones, bear more.","ttft_ms":866,"type":"result","duration_ms":3368,"uuid":"a09111e2-6dda-4d4f-a153-5c0d95fd0920","ttft_stream_ms":625,"time_to_request_ms":69,"first_content_frame_ms":625,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
